PART E -- the mass-to-light lever, and the split that exposes it
========================================================================================================================
    Ups_d    N     a_0 all     gas-dom    star-dom  split dex  TLS slope    trend
      0.3  122   2.086e-10   1.378e-10   3.130e-10      0.356      3.575   +0.119
      0.4  122   1.786e-10   1.253e-10   2.274e-10      0.259      3.691   +0.084
      0.5  122   1.531e-10   1.090e-10   1.871e-10      0.235      3.783   +0.057
      0.6  122   1.381e-10   1.057e-10   1.589e-10      0.177      3.858   +0.037
      0.7  122   1.264e-10   9.125e-11   1.404e-10      0.187      3.921   +0.020
      0.8  122   1.139e-10   1.123e-10   1.251e-10      0.047      3.975   +0.006
      0.9  122   1.066e-10   1.084e-10   1.101e-10      0.007      4.022   -0.006
      1.1  122   9.152e-11   9.552e-11   9.296e-11     -0.012      4.100   -0.024
    the lever: d log a_0 / d log Upsilon = -0.591, which is -f_star with f_star = 0.59 as it must be
    the split at the committed Upsilon = 0.5 is +0.235 dex between 52 star-dominated (f_gas < 0.3) and 20 gas-dominated (f_gas > 0.7) galaxies
    the split closes at Upsilon ~ 0.9 and the slope reaches 4 at Upsilon ~ 0.9
  [PASS] 105-E BUG PATTERN 5, and it fires hardest here: the BTFR a_0 meter's own internal consistency is a statement about the stellar mass-to-light ratio.  At the committed Upsilon = 0.5 the gas-dominated and star-dominated halves disagree by 0.24 dex; they agree, and the slope becomes 4, only at Upsilon ~ 0.9.  That is 0.25 dex above item 76's deep-tail value (0.50-0.66) and 0.25 dex above stellar populations' 0.5 +- 0.1 -- so two of the framework's own Upsilon-predictors disagree by 0.13-0.25 dex in Upsilon, and the meter cannot be trusted below that gap   (split at Upsilon = 0.5 is +0.235 dex, closes at Upsilon ~ 0.9; TLS slope 4 at Upsilon ~ 0.9; lever d log a_0/d log Upsilon = -0.59)

