PART F -- the error budget, and the precision the meter actually reaches
========================================================================================================================
  term                                                dex        %   nature
  statistical (bootstrap median)                   0.0269      6.4   random
  Upsilon zero-point, +-0.10 dex (SPS)             0.0589     14.5   COHERENT
  gas mass, +-0.05 dex                             0.0205      4.8   COHERENT
  distance scale, 2%                               0.0172      4.0   COHERENT
  per-galaxy distance (median 14%)                 0.0102      2.4   random
  per-galaxy inclination (median 64 deg +- 3)      0.0040      0.9   random
  per-galaxy V_flat (median 4%)                    0.0058      1.3   random
  quadrature total (excluding C and the split)     0.0712     17.8
  the C correction itself                          0.2018     59.1   STRUCTURAL, framework-computed
  the gas/star split at Upsilon = 0.5              0.2348     71.7   INTERNAL INCONSISTENCY
  [PASS] 105-F AGAINST INTEREST -- the honest answer to 'what precision does the local BTFR zero-point reach': 6.4% statistically, 18% once the coherent mass-to-light, gas and distance-scale terms are carried, and no better than the 0.24 dex by which the meter disagrees with itself between its gas-rich and star-rich halves.  The item's 5% is reached only in the one term that does not matter   (statistical 0.0269 dex; coherent total 0.0712 dex = 18%; internal split 0.235 dex = 72%)

========================================================================================================================
PART G -- the Newtonian and LambdaCDM alternatives computed beside the framework
========================================================================================================================
  NEWTON: with baryons only and no modification there is no asymptotically flat velocity at all -- v ~ R^{-1/2}
  beyond the disc -- so 'the BTFR zero-point' is not defined.  The relation's existence is already the result;
  its zero-point is the second-order question this item is about.
  LambdaCDM: the BTFR is inherited from M_b(M_200) and V_flat(M_200).  V_200 ~ M_200^{1/3} exactly, so a slope of
  4 requires M_b ~ M_200^{4/3}, i.e. the baryon fraction must RISE as M_200^{1/3} over four decades, and the
  relation must stay straight while abundance matching's M_*/M_200 turns over at both ends.  The measurable
  consequences are a curvature and a scatter:
     curvature measured here: -0.355 +- 0.376 -- consistent with a straight line, as the framework
     requires, but the test is UNDERPOWERED: the 2-sigma bound is |curvature| < 0.75, wide enough to admit a
     substantially bent relation, so straightness is not yet a discriminant on this sample.
     observed vertical scatter 0.241 dex, of which Lelli+2016b attribute ~0.10 dex to intrinsic scatter;
     the framework requires ZERO intrinsic scatter in a_0 and therefore has to spend all 0.10 dex on M/L, gas and distance.
  [PASS] 105-G the framework's two structural BTFR predictions -- a straight line and no intrinsic scatter -- are not violated, but the straightness test is UNDERPOWERED here and is reported as such; what does NOT survive is slope 4 at the committed Upsilon.  LambdaCDM's freedom is the other way round: it can fit any slope but has to arrange the straightness and the tightness by hand   (curvature -0.355 +- 0.376 (0.9 sigma from straight); slope 3.78 +- 0.09 (2.5 sigma from 4))

========================================================================================================================
PART H -- what this means for the decisive z ~ 2.5 measurement
========================================================================================================================
  The decisive test is a deep-MOND BTFR zero-point at z ~ 2.5 to +-0.13 dex (framework 0.00, LambdaCDM-native +0.33).  What this validation says it needs:
    1. velocities on a genuinely FLAT outer curve.  At one R_e the structural factor C is +0.31 dex (RC100's own epsilon and y),
       against +0.20 dex on a local flat curve, so +0.11 dex does NOT cancel between the two ends -- 87% of the whole budget.
    2. MEASURED gas, not scaling-relation gas.  The local coherent gas term is only 0.021 dex because M_HI is
       measured; the repo's own MUSE-DARK II refit carries +-0.20 dex from modelled gas alone, which already
       exceeds 0.13 dex on its own.
    3. a differential design.  The Upsilon zero-point (0.059 dex) cancels between z = 0 and z = 2.5 only if the
       SAME Upsilon convention is used at both ends; what does NOT cancel is the EVOLUTION of Upsilon, and the
       gas/star split found in Part E says the local Upsilon itself is uncertain by ~0.25 dex.
    4. N: with the local per-galaxy spread of 0.24 dex, reaching 0.13 dex statistically needs only N ~ 7 galaxies
       with the coherent terms already at 0.062 dex -- so N is NOT the binding constraint; the gas and
       the mass-to-light ratio are.
  [PASS] 105-H the forecast, stated in the item's own currency: the +-0.13 dex decisive measurement is not limited by sample size (a handful of galaxies would do it statistically) but by two systematics this local validation measures -- the structural factor C if the velocities are not on a flat curve, and the gas plus mass-to-light budget, which locally already contributes more than half the target   (coherent M/L + gas floor 0.062 dex of a 0.13 dex target; N needed statistically 7; non-cancelling C between R_e and a flat curve +0.11 dex)

