PART I -- mutation controls
========================================================================================================================
     M1 shuffle M_b against V_flat: sd(log a_0) goes 0.241 -> 1.119 dex, median 1.531e-10 -> 1.626e-10
  [PASS] M105-1 shuffling which baryonic mass belongs to which rotation speed destroys the meter: the spread rises by more than a factor 3, so the 0.24 dex the real data show is a real relation and not an artefact of the ranges   (real sd 0.241 dex vs shuffled 1.119 dex)
     closure: injected a_0 = 9.360e-11 with 0.00 dex of BTFR scatter -> recovered 9.360e-11 (-0.0000 dex), TLS slope 4.000
     closure: injected a_0 = 9.360e-11 with 0.24 dex of BTFR scatter -> recovered 9.119e-11 (-0.0113 dex), TLS slope 4.161
     closure: injected a_0 = 1.130e-10 with 0.00 dex of BTFR scatter -> recovered 1.130e-10 (+0.0000 dex), TLS slope 4.000
     closure: injected a_0 = 1.130e-10 with 0.24 dex of BTFR scatter -> recovered 1.143e-10 (+0.0051 dex), TLS slope 4.192
  [PASS] M105-2 closure: a synthetic BTFR of slope exactly 4 at a known a_0 is recovered exactly with no scatter and to better than 0.05 dex with SPARC's own 0.24 dex of scatter -- so the estimator itself is sound and everything found above is in the data, not in the arithmetic   (noiseless recovery -4.82e-17 dex with slope 4.000000; with 0.24 dex scatter -0.0113 dex)

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT (item 105).  The BTFR zero-point is a usable a_0 meter only with two corrections that item 105 asked to
have measured before the z ~ 2.5 test is designed, and this script measures both:
  * STRUCTURAL.  V_flat^4 = G M_b a_0 is exact only asymptotically.  On SPARC's own outermost points it overstates
    a_0 by +0.20 dex; at one effective radius, where high-z curves are sampled, RC100's own
    epsilon and y make it +0.31 dex, so +0.11 dex does not cancel between the two ends.
    Removing it with the kernel gives a_0 = 9.82e-11, canonical to 0.02 dex -- but the same estimator moves
    0.08 dex between R_eff and the last point, so this does not decide a footing.
  * MASS-TO-LIGHT.  At Upsilon = 0.5 the meter's gas-rich and star-rich halves disagree by 0.23 dex and the BTFR
    slope is 3.78, not 4.  Both close at Upsilon ~ 0.9, which contradicts item 76 and stellar populations.
  * PRECISION.  6.4% statistical on 122 galaxies; 18% with the coherent terms; 72% if the internal split
    is taken as the systematic it is.  The item's 5% is reached only in the statistical term.
  * FOR z ~ 2.5.  N is not the constraint (about 7 galaxies suffice statistically).  Measured gas, a flat
    outer curve and a shared Upsilon convention are.  A z ~ 2.5 zero-point built on scaling-relation gas cannot
    reach +-0.13 dex however many galaxies it has.
